                "explanation": "<strong>D</strong> <p>Use long division to\r                                 divide <em>x</em><sup>5</sup> – 4<em>x</em><sup>4</sup> – 2<em>x</em> + 10\r                                 by –2<em>x</em><sup>2</sup> – <em>x</em> – 6. The result is <img src=\"images/81810b3305ac423ca707e84e27c23ba0.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\"> This means the function takes on the properties of a\r                                 negative cubic function as shown below.</p>  <img src=\"images/chapter29_a-091.png\" class=\"exam-img\" style=\"max-width:100%; vertical-align:middle; margin:4px 0;\">     <p>If you take the limit as\r                                     <em>x</em> approaches −∞, the function approaches +∞. Therefore\r                                 “nonexistent” is the best choice.</p> <p>Therefore, (D) is\r                                 correct. (A) incorrectly divides the constants at the end of the\r                                 polynomial. (B) is the quotient of the leading coefficients. This\r                                 approach should be used only if the degree of the numerator equals\r                                 the degree of the denominator. (C) would be correct if there was a\r                                 horizontal asymptote at <em>y</em> = 0, which happens when the degree\r                                 of the denominator is more than the degree of the numerator.</p>"
